{"name":"Alpha Gold","hex":"#ae8e5f","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"alpha","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The name of the first letter of the Greek alphabet (Α,  α), followed by beta. In the Latin alphabet it is the predecessor to A.","Latin alpha: the Latin letter Ɑ (minuscule: ɑ)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Designates the first in an order of precedence.","Exhibiting characteristics of the alpha male/female archetype."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alpha"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alpha","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha ALF-ə is the first letter of the Greek alphabet. In the system of Greek numerals, it has a value of one. Alpha is derived from the Phoenician letter aleph ⟨𐤀⟩, whose name comes from the West Semitic word for 'ox'. Letters that arose from alpha include the Latin letter ⟨A⟩ and the Cyrillic letter ⟨А⟩."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Greek_letter_alpha_on_font_lines.svg/330px-Greek_letter_alpha_on_font_lines.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha","wikidata":"Q9887"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg/500px-K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg/1280px-K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg/500px-K%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stained glass featuring Alpha and Omega in the Königsberg in Bayern Marienkirche [de]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AK%C3%B6nigsberg_Marienkirche_-_Fenster_1a_AlphaOmega.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/500px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/1280px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/500px